<LessonLayout title="Catechist's Guide &mdash; God is Father" cic="CCC 198-267; 2779-2785" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Catechist's Guide</h2>
<p class="big-question">For class preparation and delivery. For the catechist's use only.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Section 1 &mdash; Class Summary</h3>

<p><strong>Estimated duration:</strong> 60 minutes</p>
<p><strong>Central theme:</strong> God reveals himself as Father in Scripture, and Jesus teaches us to relate to him with filial trust&mdash;not as a distant judge but as Abba, Daddy. This truth transforms how we understand prayer, sin, suffering, and our relationship with every other person.</p>
<p><strong>Learning objectives:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Understand that God's name (&laquo;I AM&raquo;) reveals his being and his closeness.</li>
<li>Discover that God is Father from the Old Testament onward&mdash;including maternal images that enrich this truth.</li>
<li>Grasp the radical novelty of Jesus: God is Abba, Daddy, and we are invited into that intimacy.</li>
<li>Interiorize that &laquo;God is Love&raquo; (1 Jn 4:8) as the foundation of all Christian doctrine.</li>
<li>Live out filial trust as children of God, not as slaves to fear.</li>
<li>Approach the Our Father as Jesus' own gift&mdash;the prayer that places his words on our lips.</li>
<li>Understand how wounds from human fatherhood can be healed by divine fatherhood.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Key verse:</strong> &laquo;For you did not receive a spirit of slavery to fall back into fear, but you received a spirit of adoption, through which we cry, 'Abba, Father!'&raquo; (Rom 8:15)</p>
<p><strong>CCC references:</strong> 198-267; 2779-2785 (primary), plus 295-296 (creation) and 301-314 (providence and evil)</p>

</section>

